Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Hopscotch Slate Swallows the Zinc
- Story & Context: When police flood-skiffs swept the lower tenth floor of Spire 44 with halogen searchlights, Sister Kael refused to cut the rooftop sound system, instead striking her copper bell against the rusted balustrade to establish an ironclad 86-BPM stride. Bram played a circular two-chord folk motif as two hundred rain-soaked dancers counted out chalked hopscotch squares across the flooded zinc plating, shouting the hook to drown out the patrol megaphones. "Draw the square before the ocean steals your toes," Kael recited through cupped hands, turning an evasion drill into the genre's first certified park-jam anthem.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Artistic high-contrast photograph of chalk hopscotch numbers drawn on wet zinc rooftop metal under cold rain, water puddles reflecting searchlights, an acoustic guitar lying beside an antique cowbell, lyric text subtly inscribed into the water rings: "Draw the square before the ocean steals your toes."

Track 2: Who Told You The Water Counted As Base?
- Story & Context: Recorded during a gale in the gutted boiler room of an abandoned midtown luxury spire, this track captures Malik Chen’s tearful confession over the sharp, unyielding ping of a hand-tuned cowbell. Malik admitted into an open ribbon mic that he cut his cousin's salvage towline during the Great Breach of '88 to save his own cassette deck and notebooks, his delivery sliding seamlessly between rapid-fire golden-age couplets and trembling, naked folk singing. His voice cracks against the guitar's open D-drone as he delivers the central accusation: "You can’t call safety on what already learned to drown."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Intimate cinematic portrait of a young man weeping while rapping into a vintage ribbon mic inside a decaying concrete control room, water dripping from the ceiling into copper pots, warm amber battery lamp glow, dark minimalist album cover aesthetic.

Track 3: Phase-Cancel the Drowned Cathedral
- Story & Context: In the aftermath of the raid that scattered Archipelago 82, the trio rowed out to the partially exposed limestone belfry of St. Jude-in-the-Swamp to record one final, unbroken twenty-minute performance at sunrise. Using the natural cavernous reverb of the flooded nave beneath their skiff, Jamilah struck two out-of-tune cowbells against each other until their acoustic overtones canceled out into an eerie, shimmering drone. As the sun cut through the salt haze, Kael whispered the legend of the first block parties held on dry land, singing the closing refrain: "The rhythm never drowned, it just grew fins and learned to pray."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Surreal, haunting image of a wooden rowboat tied to the stone gargoyles of a submerged gothic cathedral tower at pale sunrise, mist rising from the ocean surface, solitary figure standing in the bow holding a pair of oxidized bells, minimalist fine-art photography style.

